WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.083 --> 00:00:03.294
So I wanna start by offering you a free,

00:00:03.294 --> 00:00:04.921
no-tech life hack

00:00:04.921 --> 00:00:06.965
and all it requires of you is this

00:00:06.965 --> 00:00:10.427
that you change your
posture for two minutes.

00:00:10.427 --> 00:00:12.595
Do you remember the power pose craze

00:00:12.595 --> 00:00:14.055
from about a decade ago?

00:00:14.055 --> 00:00:16.641
In the second most popular TED Talk ever,

00:00:16.641 --> 00:00:20.770
psychologist Amy Cuddy told
over 60 million viewers

00:00:20.770 --> 00:00:22.939
that they can change their life outcomes

00:00:22.939 --> 00:00:25.275
by simply changing their body language.

00:00:25.275 --> 00:00:28.486
Tiny tweaks can lead to big changes.

00:00:28.486 --> 00:00:31.823
This is two minutes, two minutes,
two minutes, two minutes.

00:00:31.823 --> 00:00:34.159
Cuddy sparked a movement supposedly rooted

00:00:34.159 --> 00:00:36.995
in science around the idea
that changing your posture

00:00:36.995 --> 00:00:39.164
can change everything around you.

00:00:39.164 --> 00:00:42.167
By striking power poses
in tough situations,

00:00:42.167 --> 00:00:45.378
women could even gain equality
in corporate board rooms.

00:00:45.378 --> 00:00:47.464
The problem is none of it was true,

00:00:47.464 --> 00:00:49.340
says science writer Jesse Singal

00:00:49.340 --> 00:00:52.218
even according to Cuddy's own research.

00:00:52.218 --> 00:00:53.720
One of Cuddy's two co-authors,

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:56.723
a woman named Dana Carney
who's at UC Berkeley.

00:00:56.723 --> 00:00:58.725
She posted a thing to her
faculty website saying,

00:00:58.725 --> 00:01:01.603
I think we made some methodological
errors in this study.

00:01:01.603 --> 00:01:03.813
I think the effect is totally false.

00:01:03.813 --> 00:01:06.566
So even one of the people
who discovered power posing

00:01:06.566 --> 00:01:08.902
has come out and said,
there's nothing there.

00:01:08.902 --> 00:01:10.862
Singal is the author of, 'The Quick Fix

00:01:10.862 --> 00:01:14.657
"Why Fad Psychology Can't
Cure Our Social Ills'

00:01:14.657 --> 00:01:15.492
Which looks at how,

00:01:15.492 --> 00:01:17.077
academics and policy makers

00:01:17.077 --> 00:01:20.413
routinely oversell their pet projects

00:01:20.413 --> 00:01:24.501
as the one easy way to address
some major social problem.

00:01:24.501 --> 00:01:26.795
The goal of this book
is to explain to people

00:01:26.795 --> 00:01:31.007
why we keep falling for the
ideas that psychologists tell us

00:01:31.007 --> 00:01:33.551
about the ways they
gonna help fix society.

00:01:33.551 --> 00:01:37.764
And these ideas have
had a really good time

00:01:37.764 --> 00:01:38.681
in the 21st century.

00:01:38.681 --> 00:01:39.724
They've gone viral.

00:01:39.724 --> 00:01:42.060
TED-Talks culture is doing quite well.

00:01:42.060 --> 00:01:43.436
Over and over and over,

00:01:43.436 --> 00:01:45.688
I've noticed that these ideas
will burst onto the scene,

00:01:45.688 --> 00:01:48.942
they'll offer some incredible
new way to fight racism

00:01:48.942 --> 00:01:50.527
or to improve education

00:01:50.527 --> 00:01:52.904
or to improve gender
equity in the workplace.

00:01:52.904 --> 00:01:54.989
There's a rush of attention.

00:01:54.989 --> 00:01:56.699
Often a rush of research dollars.

00:01:56.699 --> 00:01:57.951
Everyone gets really into them.

00:01:57.951 --> 00:02:00.870
There's the NPR and New
York Times coverage.

00:02:00.870 --> 00:02:03.123
And then a few years later,
more research comes out.

00:02:03.123 --> 00:02:07.335
We realized the idea
was barely true if that,

00:02:07.335 --> 00:02:09.379
and it ends up having wasted a lot of time

00:02:09.379 --> 00:02:11.297
Singal Points to other fad ideas

00:02:11.297 --> 00:02:13.383
that have had long lasting impacts,

00:02:13.383 --> 00:02:14.717
the self-esteem movement

00:02:14.717 --> 00:02:16.719
which sought to boost academic achievement

00:02:16.719 --> 00:02:18.847
by making students feel
good about themselves,

00:02:18.847 --> 00:02:20.390
regardless of performance

00:02:20.390 --> 00:02:22.142
dominated public school curriculum

00:02:22.142 --> 00:02:23.768
starting in the 1980s.

00:02:23.768 --> 00:02:27.230
This idea was championed by a
powerful California legislator

00:02:27.230 --> 00:02:29.732
who believes that praising
kids and even criminals

00:02:29.732 --> 00:02:31.985
would help them to overcome obstacles.

00:02:31.985 --> 00:02:34.696
The problem is the claim
that improving self-esteem

00:02:34.696 --> 00:02:36.990
can fix all these
negative societal outcomes

00:02:36.990 --> 00:02:39.117
which there was never
really any evidence for it.

00:02:39.117 --> 00:02:40.326
There's not a harm to like,

00:02:40.326 --> 00:02:43.246
making people feel like
they're loved to have worth.

00:02:43.246 --> 00:02:45.582
The harm is like partly opportunity costs.

00:02:45.582 --> 00:02:46.875
I think with a lot of these ideas

00:02:46.875 --> 00:02:49.294
people really like get a
little bit fixated on them

00:02:49.294 --> 00:02:51.129
at the expense of other
more important things.

00:02:51.129 --> 00:02:54.215
So if you really think
criminals are committing crimes

00:02:54.215 --> 00:02:56.509
as a result of low self-esteem,

00:02:56.509 --> 00:02:58.761
that might distract you from actual things

00:02:58.761 --> 00:03:00.263
that contribute to high crime rates.

00:03:00.263 --> 00:03:02.390
Singal says, one of the most influential

00:03:02.390 --> 00:03:04.100
and pernicious quick fixes

00:03:04.100 --> 00:03:06.019
has to do with implicit bias

00:03:06.019 --> 00:03:08.313
especially with regard to racism.

00:03:08.313 --> 00:03:11.649
He points to the Implicit
Association Test or IAT ,

00:03:11.649 --> 00:03:14.319
a free online quiz that supposedly shows

00:03:14.319 --> 00:03:16.696
how takers are unconsciously biased

00:03:16.696 --> 00:03:19.657
with regard to overweight
people, gays women,

00:03:19.657 --> 00:03:22.368
and especially blacks
and other minorities.

00:03:22.368 --> 00:03:24.787
The test received glowing media attention

00:03:24.787 --> 00:03:27.582
from the likes of Malcolm
Gladwell and Nicholas Kristof.

00:03:27.582 --> 00:03:28.750
It routinely used

00:03:28.750 --> 00:03:31.294
in all sorts of diversity
training programs

00:03:31.294 --> 00:03:34.380
and educational settings from
K through 12 through college,

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:36.341
yet there's at least two major problems

00:03:36.341 --> 00:03:38.134
with the IAT says Singal.

00:03:38.134 --> 00:03:40.470
You got very different tasks on the IAT

00:03:40.470 --> 00:03:44.015
in a short, period if you
retake it, that's one problem.

00:03:44.015 --> 00:03:45.225
The other problem is validity,

00:03:45.225 --> 00:03:46.643
which is basically proof

00:03:46.643 --> 00:03:49.395
that the test is measuring
something out in the real world.

00:03:49.395 --> 00:03:51.564
So the claim for a long time was that

00:03:52.815 --> 00:03:55.109
you get a high score
on the Black white IAT

00:03:55.109 --> 00:03:58.821
that predicts you're going to
discriminate against people.

00:03:58.821 --> 00:04:00.531
It doesn't seem to really,

00:04:00.531 --> 00:04:02.200
except in the tiniest way.

00:04:02.200 --> 00:04:03.993
I mean, it's statistically significant

00:04:03.993 --> 00:04:06.871
but it's almost certainly not
relevant to the real world.

00:04:06.871 --> 00:04:08.331
The best estimate we have is that

00:04:08.331 --> 00:04:13.336
your IAT score, IAT score
explained about 1% of the variance

00:04:13.628 --> 00:04:16.381
in people's discriminatory
behavior in lab settings

00:04:16.381 --> 00:04:18.841
which is just a fancy way of saying that

00:04:18.841 --> 00:04:20.051
it predicts very well.

00:04:20.051 --> 00:04:21.386
One of the goals of 'The Quick Fix'

00:04:21.386 --> 00:04:23.721
is to make people more
aware of our willingness

00:04:23.721 --> 00:04:26.641
to believe that, all our
problems can be cured easily

00:04:26.641 --> 00:04:28.851
by focusing on one thing.

00:04:28.851 --> 00:04:31.104
Yeah I think humans just
by dint of our brains

00:04:31.104 --> 00:04:33.106
we're always can be susceptible to

00:04:33.106 --> 00:04:35.608
less than rigorous mana causal accounts.

00:04:35.608 --> 00:04:37.151
So like power posing was like,

00:04:37.151 --> 00:04:39.487
Oh, we just got to make
women feel more powerful.

00:04:39.487 --> 00:04:40.947
They never bothered to prove that

00:04:40.947 --> 00:04:43.324
that's really, what's
driving these discrepancies.

00:04:43.324 --> 00:04:45.576
I think these ideas like take these big,

00:04:45.576 --> 00:04:47.829
complicated, tangled problems

00:04:47.829 --> 00:04:50.623
and just zoom in on a little bit of it

00:04:50.623 --> 00:04:51.958
and it would be nice

00:04:51.958 --> 00:04:53.918
if addressing that bit
could address a whole thing

00:04:53.918 --> 00:04:55.253
but there's often not a lot of evidence.

00:04:55.253 --> 00:04:56.045
That's the case.

